Jen's phone was dying, and she didn't have any juice to read the rest of the message. She rattled the doorknob, but it was locked; when a cancellation happened the teachers herded the kids into every classroom and locked all the doors until the parents came to get them. She could see out the front window that the street looked the same, but there were a dozen emergency vehicles parked at the stop sign across the street and a hundred kids in their pajamas pressed against the chain-link fence around Cushing's Park.

After half an hour officials came by in pairs and told Jen the concert was off, said, "Nothing we can do, ma'am," and told her to go home.

Jen got home in the dark, cleaned up, opened a book, then closed it again. She called her parents and listened to them de-ice their windshield.

The next morning Theo woke up crying and said he had to sing. Jen told him she really tried to get in, but he could sing "Let It Snow" into a tape recorder if he wanted to, or they could mess around with Audacity again. But he just wanted to sing into the air.

They stood in the kitchen, Jen washing the breakfast dishes. Theo sang quietly and didn't stutter.

